    <li>
     <a class="hover:text-[#c4924a]" href="/">
      Home
     </a>
    </li>
    <li class="flex items-center gap-2">
     <span aria-hidden="true">
      /
     </span>
     <span aria-current="page">
      Specialties
     </span>
    </li>
    <li class="flex items-center gap-2">
     <span aria-hidden="true">
      /
     </span>
     <span aria-current="page">
      Shoulder Pain
     </span>
    </li>
   </ol>
  </nav>

  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline>
   {"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://ryansrolfing.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Specialties"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Shoulder Pain"}]}
  </script>

  <article class="px-5 py-20 md:py-28">
   <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.28em] text-[#c4924a]">
     Shoulder pain and how the arm is carried
    </p>
    <h1 class="mt-5 text-5xl leading-tight md:text-7xl">
     Rolfing for Shoulder Pain in Folsom
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9 text-[#545454]/85">
     The shoulder moves a lot, and it depends on the ribs, neck, and shoulder blade for a place to move from. When that support is missing, the shoulder often grips.
    </p>
   </div>
   <div class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-3xl space-y-12 text-lg leading-9 text-[#545454]/85">
    <section>
     <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight text-[#545454]">
      Why the shoulder gets overloaded
     </h2>
     <p class="mt-5">
      Reaching, desk work, driving, lifting, and old injuries can leave the shoulder blade stuck to the rib cage, or the neck doing the shoulder’s job. Pain at the top of the shoulder, the front of the joint, or between the shoulder blades often belongs to that larger pattern.
     </p>
     <p class="mt-5">
      Rolfing looks at those relationships instead of only rubbing the sore spot. Neck tension, jaw clenching, and headache patterns frequently sit in the same neighborhood.
     </p>
    </section>
    <section>
     <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight text-[#545454]">
      What a session may include
     </h2>
     <p class="mt-5">
      Ryan may work with the shoulder blade, chest, ribs, neck, arm, and sometimes the low back or pelvis if one side is hiking to hold the arm up. A simple reach or a breath can show whether the shoulder can move without the neck joining in.
     </p>
     <p class="mt-5">
      Pressure is collaborative. The goal is not to crank the joint through a stretch.
     </p>
    </section>
    <section>
     <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight text-[#545454]">
      What this work does not do
     </h2>
     <p class="mt-5">
      Rolfing is not medical care for a rotator cuff tear, dislocation, fracture, or infection. It does not rebuild a torn tendon. If the shoulder is hot, swollen, unstable, or suddenly weak after an injury, see a doctor before bodywork.
     </p>
     <p class="mt-5">
      For recurring strain tied to posture and how the arm is carried, Rolfing can be a practical complement to other care. It is not a promise that the pain will be gone.
     </p>
    </section>
   </div>
   <section class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-4xl rounded-[2rem] bg-[#545454] p-8 text-white md:p-10">
    <h2 class="text-4xl text-white">
     This work may fit if you notice
    </h2>
    <ul class="mt-7 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-white/82">
     <li>
      Shoulder tension that returns after massage or stretching.
     </li>
     <li>
      Pain linked with desk work, driving, lifting, or sleeping on one side.
     </li>
     <li>
      Neck, jaw, or upper-back tension that shows up with the shoulder.
     </li>
     <li>
      A shoulder that feels hiked, rounded forward, or stuck.
     </li>
    </ul>
   </section>
   <section class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-3xl">
    <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight">
     Questions people ask
    </h2>
    <div class="mt-8 space-y-4">
     <details class="rounded-2xl bg-[#f8f6f2] p-6">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-2xl text-[#545454]">
       Can Rolfing help shoulder pain?
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-[#545454]/85">
       Rolfing may support some people with recurring shoulder strain by working with the shoulder blade, ribs, neck, chest, and the way the arm is carried. It is not medical treatment for tears, dislocation, or joint disease.
      </p>
     </details>
     <details class="rounded-2xl bg-[#f8f6f2] p-6">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-2xl text-[#545454]">
       Will you only work on the shoulder?
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-[#545454]/85">
       Usually no. Shoulder pain often involves the neck, ribs, chest, upper back, and sometimes the pelvis. Ryan works with the pattern that is keeping the shoulder overloaded.
      </p>
     </details>
     <details class="rounded-2xl bg-[#f8f6f2] p-6">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-2xl text-[#545454]">
       What if I also have neck pain or jaw tension?
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-[#545454]/85">
       Neck pain, jaw tension, and shoulder strain often travel together. The <a class="text-[#c4924a] underline-offset-4 hover:underline" href="/rolfing-for-neck-pain-folsom/">neck</a> and <a class="text-[#c4924a] underline-offset-4 hover:underline" href="/rolfing-for-jaw-pain-tmj-folsom/">jaw</a> pages describe that overlap. A session can include more than one of them when they are part of the same pattern.
      </p>
     </details>
     <details class="rounded-2xl bg-[#f8f6f2] p-6">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer text-2xl text-[#545454]">
       When should I see a doctor first?
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-8 text-[#545454]/85">
       Seek medical care first for sudden shoulder pain after an injury, inability to lift the arm, visible deformity, significant swelling, fever, or numbness and weakness that is new or worsening.
      </p>
     </details>
    </div>
   </section>
   <section class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-3xl space-y-6 text-lg leading-9 text-[#545454]/85">
    <h2 class="text-4xl leading-tight text-[#545454]">
     Shoulder pain bodywork in Folsom
    </h2>
    <p>
     Ryan’s Rolfing is located at 101 Parkshore Drive, Folsom, CA 95630, and serves clients from Folsom, El Dorado Hills, Shingle Springs, Cameron Park, Placerville, and the greater Sacramento area.
    </p>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4">
     <a class="inline-block rounded-full bg-[#c4924a] px-8 py-4 text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-white hover:bg-[#9b7139]" href="https://rolfing-106793.square.site">
      Book a session
     </a>
     <a class="inline-block rounded-full border border-[#c4924a] px-8 py-4 text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-[#c4924a] hover:bg-[#f8f6f2]" href="/rolfing-for-neck-pain-folsom/">
      Neck pain support
     </a>
     <a class="inline-block rounded-full border border-[#c4924a] px-8 py-4 text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-[#c4924a] hover:bg-[#f8f6f2]" href="/serving/folsom-rolfing/">
      Folsom office
     </a>
    </div>
    <p>
     Related:
     <a class="text-[#c4924a] underline-offset-4 hover:underline" href="/rolfing-for-jaw-pain-tmj-folsom/">jaw and TMJ tension</a>,
     <a class="text-[#c4924a] underline-offset-4 hover:underline" href="/rolfing-for-headaches-migraines-folsom/">headaches and migraines</a>,
     <a class="text-[#c4924a] underline-offset-4 hover:underline" href="/rolfing-for-back-pain-folsom/">low back pain</a>.
    </p>
   </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
